Key Design Decisions
No third-party SDK: AirTALK uses raw browser WebRTC APIs, keeping full control and avoiding per-minute SDK costs.
WebSocket as signaling backbone: A single persistent WS connection handles everything — auth, matchmaking, SDP relay, ICE relay, presence, and system messages. This is efficient and avoids REST polling.
P2P media flow: Once the WebRTC connection is established, voice audio flows directly between the two browsers (peer-to-peer), not through AirTALK's servers — this dramatically reduces server bandwidth costs and latency.
TURN for NAT traversal: The self-hosted TURN server (coturn on Hetzner) handles cases where direct P2P fails due to symmetric NATs or firewalls. Time-limited HMAC credentials prevent credential abuse.
